We are starting a timed AI-assisted development candidate exercise.

IMPORTANT: Do not modify any files yet. This first phase is requirements analysis only.

Exercise constraints:

- Browser-based HTML, CSS and JavaScript.
- 3x3 grid.
- Two human players, X and O.
- Alternating turns.
- The game must never end in a draw.
- Every possible line of play must terminate.
- No build step.
- No backend, database or network calls.
- Current Chrome is sufficient.
- We eventually need docs/RULES.md and docs/DESIGN.md.
- Commit history and raw AI session transcripts are part of the assessment.
- We have a maximum of 3 hours active work, so prefer a small, defensible implementation over unnecessary features.

Our current proposed rule is:

1. Normal tic-tac-toe three-in-a-row wins immediately.
2. A move may only be made in an empty square; an invalid click does not consume the turn.
3. If all 9 squares become occupied without a three-in-a-row winner, the player with fewer marks wins.
4. Since X moves first, a full board contains 5 X marks and 4 O marks, so in that case O wins via the "Komi" / tie-break rule.
5. After a terminal result, no further moves are accepted.
6. The UI should announce whether X won, O won, or O won via Komi.
7. The user can start another game after the result.

Before coding:

- Inspect the existing repository.
- Restate the acceptance criteria.
- Identify ambiguities in the brief and in our proposed rule.
- Evaluate whether this rule really guarantees both no-draw and termination.
- Identify any important edge cases.
- Briefly consider at least two alternative rule sets and explain why they may be worse or better for this exercise.
- Recommend the minimal implementation structure.
- Recommend how we can validate the no-draw and termination properties, preferably including an exhaustive game-tree check if it can stay simple.
- Point out anything in our current idea that should be changed.

Do not edit files yet.
Stop after the analysis and wait for approval.

